// The certifying generator's CERTIFICATE FOLD. Design:
// docs/certgen-cert-expression-design.md.
//
// The transcriber (lib/certgen_code.hthr) turns a fn's code into an `Ir`.
// This file turns that `Ir` into a CERTIFICATE EXPRESSION: a first-order
// description of the chain of ∃-composition-kit applications that proves
// `Converges(prog, env, ir, out)` for it — one constructor per kit lemma
// (`ret_conv`, `letcon_conv`, `letcall_conv`, `letmatch_conv` in
// lib/ir_v1.hthr), carrying the arguments a rendered application needs from
// the IR side. The proof TEXT is rendered from Rust
// (crates/elab/src/certgen.rs), because every remaining argument is a *name*:
// the generated program, the source parameters the environment is spelled
// with, the emitted `gk`/`gih`/`tail` binders, the callee invariants'
// theorem names. Design doc §3 is that split, stated once.
//
// Ordinary program-layer Heather — inductives and `fn`s, no `match code`, no
// layer-m content at all, so nothing here pays the 52-branch `rec_ga` gate.
// Elaborated on top of `lib/ir_v1.hthr`'s declarations prefix: `Ir`, `Refs`
// and `pick_arm` come from there, and this file declares no import and
// redeclares nothing.
//
// THE CONVENTIONS IT INHERITS, none of which it may restate differently:
//
// 1. CALLEE NUMBERING (lib/certgen_code.hthr convention 3). The `Ir` this
//    file folds is in the WALKER's coordinate system: callee 0 is the
//    enclosing function itself — the structural self-call, whose certificate
//    is the induction hypothesis — and a tag `c ≥ 1` is the `c`-th distinct
//    foreign callee. The program-closure indices the emitted certificate
//    actually spells are computed downstream, from names, exactly as the IR's
//    own are. So `CIh` versus `CCall` is decided HERE, on the tag, and the
//    readback only renders the choice.
//
// 2. THE BODY SHAPE (docs/certifying-generator-design.md; the fragment's "at
//    most one match, at the root" rule). A recursive body is
//    `LetMatch(scrut, Arm(zero, Arm(succ, NoArm)), Ret(RCons(0, RNil)))` —
//    the two `Nat` arms and the rebinding continuation — and anything else is
//    a straight-line body. The emitted proof is an induction over the
//    scrutinized parameter with `letmatch_conv` applied INSIDE each arm, which
//    is why `CRec` carries two whole certificates rather than one.
//
// 3. THE CHAIN IS ITS OWN IR (design doc §2). Three kit lemmas take the
//    remaining region as an argument (`letcon_conv`'s and `letcall_conv`'s
//    `rest`, `letmatch_conv`'s rebinding continuation), and that region is
//    exactly what the sub-certificate is *about* — so it is not carried
//    twice. The readback prints it off the sub-certificate, which is also the
//    only way it could resolve the callee indices anyway (convention 1).
//
// The generator is untrusted (issues/certifying-generator.md): a fold that
// picks the wrong lemma or routes the induction hypothesis to a foreign call
// produces a certificate the kernel REFUSES, never an unsound theorem. That
// is what makes this file safe to be a heuristic, and what
// `crates/elab/tests/certgen_cert.rs` demonstrates with one edit to it.

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The data.

// Is the induction hypothesis in scope? A property of the POSITION, not of
// the instruction: the `Succ` arm of the root match has one, the `Zero` arm
// and a non-recursive body do not. Threaded unchanged through a chain, so a
// self-call outside a `Succ` arm declines rather than inventing a hypothesis.
inductive Ihp { IhNo, IhYes }

// One constructor per kit lemma, its fields the lemma's arguments that come
// from the IR side (design doc §2's table):
//
//   CRet(rs)                 ret_conv(p, env, rs, vs, refl)
//   CCon(tag, args, hr)      letcon_conv(p, env, tag, args, rest, fs, vs, refl, hr)
//   CIh(args, hr)            letcall_conv(p, env, 0, args, rest, cargs, ws, vs,
//                                         refl, <the induction hypothesis>, hr)
//   CCall(c, args, hr)       letcall_conv(p, env, c, args, rest, cargs, ws, vs,
//                                         refl, <callee c's invariant>, hr)
//   CMatch(scrut, ctag, ha, hr)
//                            letmatch_conv(p, env, scrut, arms, rest, ctag,
//                                          fields, ws, vs, refl, ha, hr)
//
// with every `rest` read off the sub-certificate (convention 3) and every
// remaining argument a name (design doc §3).
//
// `letcall_conv` gets two constructors because WHICH certificate discharges a
// call is the most interesting decision this file makes — the difference
// between an induction and a lie — and a constructor is what makes it
// perturbable in one edit and readable in the pins below.
//
// `CStuck` is the decline. It is *not* propagated by helper functions here
// (matching `Cert` costs elaboration time and buys nothing): a certificate
// with a stuck node anywhere is refused whole at readback, which is the same
// totality with the recognition at the rim — where `TNo`'s and `SigNo`'s
// reasons already live.
inductive Cert {
    CStuck,
    CRet(rs: Refs),
    CCon(tag: Nat, args: Refs, hr: Cert),
    CIh(args: Refs, hr: Cert),
    CCall(c: Nat, args: Refs, hr: Cert),
    CMatch(scrut: Nat, ctag: Nat, ha: Cert, hr: Cert),
}

// A whole invariant's shape: a straight-line body proves one chain; a
// recursive body proves one `letmatch_conv` per constructor arm, under the
// induction the emitted text opens. A `Nat` induction has exactly the two
// arms (`CRec`); a TIER-1 induction has one per constructor, collected in
// declaration order (`CRecW` — docs/certgen-tier1-source-types-design.md §6,
// the constructor-level edit §8.1 of the design doc predicted).
inductive CArms { CANil, CACons(c: Cert, rest: CArms) }

inductive CertOut {
    CPlain(c: Cert),
    CRec(scrut: Nat, zero: Cert, succ: Cert),
    CRecW(scrut: Nat, arms: CArms),
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The fold.

// One straight-line region: the instruction chain is the certificate chain,
// each instruction's lemma chosen by its constructor.
//
// A call to callee 0 is the structural self-call, whose certificate is the
// induction hypothesis — available only where `ih` says so. Every other
// instruction form (a nested `LetMatch`, an arm cell, the loop forms) is
// outside the fragment the emitters can prove, so it declines.
fn cert_chain(ir: Ir, ih: Ihp) → Cert {
    match ir {
        Ret(rs) => CRet(rs),
        LetCon(tag, args, rest) => CCon(tag, args, cert_chain(rest, ih)),
        LetCall(c, args, rest) =>
            match c {
                Zero => match ih {
                    IhNo => CStuck,
                    IhYes => CIh(args, cert_chain(rest, ih)),
                },
                Succ(c2) => CCall(c, args, cert_chain(rest, ih)),
            },
        _ => CStuck,
    }
}

// The rebinding continuation of the root match: the arm's single output,
// rebound as slot 0 and returned. Anything else is a body whose shape the
// emitted induction does not describe, so it declines here rather than
// producing a certificate about a different program.
fn cert_rebind(after: Ir) → Cert {
    match after {
        Ret(rs) => match rs {
            RCons(i, next) => match i {
                Zero => match next { RNil => CRet(RCons(0, RNil)), _ => CStuck },
                Succ(k) => CStuck,
            },
            _ => CStuck,
        },
        _ => CStuck,
    }
}

// The two `Nat` arms, each wrapped in its own `letmatch_conv`: the `Zero` arm
// with constructor tag 0 and no induction hypothesis, the `Succ` arm with tag
// 1 and one.
//
// The arms are selected with the library's own `pick_arm` — the very function
// `letmatch_conv`'s `ha` hypothesis is stated against — rather than by
// destructuring the arm chain here. That is both faithful (the lemma and the
// fold agree by construction on what "the arm for tag `t`" means) and cheap;
// an arm chain too short answers `NoArm`, which `cert_chain` declines.
fn cert_arms(scrut: Nat, arms: Ir, after: Ir) → CertOut {
    CRec(scrut,
        CMatch(scrut, 0, cert_chain(pick_arm(arms)(0), IhNo), cert_rebind(after)),
        CMatch(scrut, 1, cert_chain(pick_arm(arms)(1), IhYes), cert_rebind(after)))
}

// The root, and the file's entry point: the elaborator applies exactly this
// to the transcriber's `Ir` (design doc §5 — an ordinary global application,
// no analyzer slot, because nothing here is layer m).
fn cert_body(ir: Ir) → CertOut {
    match ir {
        LetMatch(scrut, arms, after) => cert_arms(scrut, arms, after),
        _ => CPlain(cert_chain(ir, IhNo)),
    }
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The TIER-1 fold (docs/certgen-tier1-source-types-design.md §6). Which arms
// have an induction hypothesis is a fact about the scrutinized DECLARATION —
// which constructors have recursive fields — so the tier-1 entry point takes
// it as a `kids` bitmask (little-endian: bit `i` set iff constructor `i` has
// children), decoded per arm. A self-call in a childless arm still declines,
// the `Zero`-arm honesty check per constructor.

fn ihp_flip(i: Ihp) → Ihp { match i { IhNo => IhYes, IhYes => IhNo } }

// Parity: the mask's bit 0.
fn kid_bit(n: Nat) → Ihp {
    match n { Zero => IhNo, Succ(m) => ihp_flip(kid_bit(m)) }
}

// Floor-halving: the mask shifted right once. One step of descent per call
// with an alternating carry, because the termination discipline wants a
// single decreasing argument — the carry rides in the returned function
// (the file-wide idiom).
fn kid_shift(n: Nat) → Ihp → Nat {
    match n {
        Zero => λ (b: Ihp) => Zero,
        Succ(m) => λ (b: Ihp) => match b {
            IhYes => Succ(kid_shift(m)(IhNo)),
            IhNo => kid_shift(m)(IhYes),
        },
    }
}

fn kid_rest(n: Nat) → Nat { kid_shift(n)(IhNo) }

// One `CMatch` per constructor arm, tags counted up in declaration order.
// The arm chain drives the recursion (it is the structural argument, passed
// twice so the moving copy can descend while `whole` stays for selection);
// the arm itself is still SELECTED through the library's own `pick_arm`, so
// the fold and `letmatch_conv`'s hypothesis agree by construction, exactly
// as `cert_arms` does for the two `Nat` arms. The tag counter and the
// remaining mask vary with the recursion, so they ride in the returned
// function.
fn cert_arms_w(scrut: Nat, whole: Ir, after: Ir, arms: Ir) → Nat → Nat → CArms {
    match arms {
        Arm(b, next) => λ (tag: Nat) (kids: Nat) => CACons(
            CMatch(scrut, tag, cert_chain(pick_arm(whole)(tag), kid_bit(kids)),
                   cert_rebind(after)),
            cert_arms_w(scrut, whole, after, next)(Succ(tag))(kid_rest(kids))),
        _ => λ (tag: Nat) (kids: Nat) => CANil,
    }
}

// The tier-1 entry point: the elaborator applies this — instead of
// `cert_body` — exactly when the scrutinized parameter is tier-1, which it
// knows from the declaration; the `Ir` alone deliberately cannot tell a
// two-constructor tier-1 match from a `Nat` match, and nothing needs it to.
fn cert_body_w(ir: Ir, kids: Nat) → CertOut {
    match ir {
        LetMatch(scrut, arms, after) =>
            CRecW(scrut, cert_arms_w(scrut, arms, after, arms)(0)(kids)),
        _ => CPlain(CStuck),
    }
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The file's own acceptance, as `lib/certgen_code.hthr` states its own: each
// right-hand side is the certificate the emitters render for the same shape,
// one layer below the readback. A divergence is a failed `refl`, here, at
// library elaboration time. The fixtures are deliberately SMALL — one
// instruction per fact — because a `refl` between two `Wf`-lowered values is
// the expensive kind of pin (issues/certgen-harness-cost.md), and the whole
// transcriptions are pinned end to end by the emissions themselves.

// The two call constructors, which is the routing decision: callee 0 under a
// `Succ` arm is the induction hypothesis…
theorem self_call_is_the_ih():
    cert_chain(LetCall(0, RNil, Ret(RCons(0, RNil))), IhYes)
    = CIh(RNil, CRet(RCons(0, RNil)))
{ refl }

// …a foreign tag is the callee's own invariant, at the tag the reflection
// table numbers it with…
theorem foreign_call_is_the_callee():
    cert_chain(LetCall(1, RNil, Ret(RCons(0, RNil))), IhYes)
    = CCall(1, RNil, CRet(RCons(0, RNil)))
{ refl }

// …and with no hypothesis in scope — a `Zero` arm, or a body with no match —
// the same self-call declines instead of borrowing one.
theorem self_call_without_the_ih_declines():
    cert_chain(LetCall(0, RNil, Ret(RCons(0, RNil))), IhNo) = CStuck
{ refl }

// A constructor instruction is `letcon_conv`, and the chain continues.
theorem con_chains():
    cert_chain(LetCon(1, RCons(0, RNil), Ret(RCons(0, RNil))), IhNo)
    = CCon(1, RCons(0, RNil), CRet(RCons(0, RNil)))
{ refl }

// A recursive body: the root match becomes two `letmatch_conv`s, one per
// constructor tag, each closing on the rebinding `Ret`. `dbl`'s shape, with
// the arms shortened to one instruction each.
theorem rec_body_splits_the_arms():
    cert_body(LetMatch(0,
        Arm(Ret(RCons(0, RNil)),
        Arm(LetCall(0, RCons(0, RNil), Ret(RCons(0, RNil))),
        NoArm)),
        Ret(RCons(0, RNil))))
    = CRec(0,
        CMatch(0, 0, CRet(RCons(0, RNil)), CRet(RCons(0, RNil))),
        CMatch(0, 1, CIh(RCons(0, RNil), CRet(RCons(0, RNil))),
               CRet(RCons(0, RNil))))
{ refl }

// A continuation that is not the rebinding `Ret` describes a different
// program from the one the emitted theorem would be about.
theorem nonrebinding_continuation_declines():
    cert_rebind(Ret(RCons(1, RNil))) = CStuck
{ refl }

// The tier-1 fold: `tsum`'s shape — kids mask 2 (constructor 1 has a
// child), so the first arm folds without a hypothesis and the second's
// self-call is the induction hypothesis.
theorem tier1_body_splits_the_arms():
    cert_body_w(LetMatch(0,
        Arm(LetCon(0, RNil, Ret(RCons(0, RNil))),
        Arm(LetCall(0, RCons(1, RNil), Ret(RCons(0, RNil))),
        NoArm)),
        Ret(RCons(0, RNil))), 2)
    = CRecW(0, CACons(
        CMatch(0, 0, CCon(0, RNil, CRet(RCons(0, RNil))), CRet(RCons(0, RNil))),
        CACons(
        CMatch(0, 1, CIh(RCons(1, RNil), CRet(RCons(0, RNil))), CRet(RCons(0, RNil))),
        CANil)))
{ refl }

// …and a self-call in a CHILDLESS arm declines, per constructor — the
// `Zero`-arm honesty check generalized: kids mask 0 makes the same arm
// stick.
theorem tier1_childless_self_call_declines():
    cert_arms_w(0,
        Arm(LetCall(0, RNil, Ret(RCons(0, RNil))), NoArm),
        Ret(RCons(0, RNil)),
        Arm(LetCall(0, RNil, Ret(RCons(0, RNil))), NoArm))(0)(0)
    = CACons(CMatch(0, 0, CStuck, CRet(RCons(0, RNil))), CANil)
{ refl }
